systemd: fall back to numeric hidepid= when procfs rejects the option string

ProtectProc= and ProcSubset= are written into a procfs mount option string by
mount_procfs(), symbolically: "hidepid=invisible", "subset=pid".  Both spellings
arrived in Linux 5.8.  Before that procfs parses hidepid= as a number and knows
no subset= at all, and it rejects the whole option string over a single element
it does not recognise, so one unknown token fails the mount with -EINVAL.

Measured on dandelion (4.9.190) inside a private mount namespace, mounting a
fresh procfs over /mnt:

  hidepid=invisible  EINVAL
  subset=pid         EINVAL
  hidepid=1          ok
  hidepid=2          ok
  (no options)       ok

So the capability is there, only under the older spelling.  This is 5 of the 20
units still failing at step NAMESPACE in stage-2 attempt 7; the other 15 are
0013.

Retry once with the numeric form when the first mount returns -EINVAL, mapping
noaccess to 1 and invisible to 2.  ptraceable has no pre-5.8 equivalent and is
mapped to 2, which is blunter than what was asked for but not weaker: hidepid=2
hides other users' processes outright rather than making them unreadable.
subset=pid is dropped, because there is nothing to translate it to; a unit that
asked for it gets a procfs with the non-process files still present, which is
what it would have got from any kernel before 5.8.

The retry is deliberately narrow.  It only runs when the first attempt failed
with exactly -EINVAL and only when options were passed at all, so a unit with
default settings mounts procfs through the unchanged path, and if the numeric
string is identical to what already failed the original error is returned rather
than mounting twice.

Upstream is unlikely to take this: systemd's baseline is far above 4.9.

--- a/src/core/namespace.c
+++ b/src/core/namespace.c
@@ -1560,6 +1560,27 @@
         return mount_private_apivfs("cgroup2", mount_entry_path(m), "/sys/fs/cgroup", /* opts= */ NULL, p->runtime_scope);
 }
 
+static const char* protect_proc_to_hidepid_numeric(ProtectProc pp) {
+
+        /* procfs only learned the symbolic hidepid= values in Linux 5.8. Before that it parses a number and
+         * rejects anything else. */
+
+        switch (pp) {
+        case PROTECT_PROC_DEFAULT:
+                return "0";
+        case PROTECT_PROC_NOACCESS:
+                return "1";
+        case PROTECT_PROC_INVISIBLE:
+                return "2";
+        case PROTECT_PROC_PTRACEABLE:
+                /* No pre-5.8 equivalent. hidepid=2 hides other users' processes outright, so it is not
+                 * weaker than what was asked for, only blunter. */
+                return "2";
+        default:
+                return NULL;
+        }
+}
+
 static int mount_procfs(const MountEntry *m, const NamespaceParameters *p) {
         _cleanup_free_ char *opts = NULL;
         int r;
@@ -1586,7 +1607,30 @@
          * one. i.e we don't reuse existing mounts here under any condition, we want a new instance owned by
          * our user namespace and with our hidepid= settings applied. Hence, let's get rid of everything
          * mounted on /proc/ first. */
-        return mount_private_apivfs("proc", mount_entry_path(m), "/proc", opts, p->runtime_scope);
+        r = mount_private_apivfs("proc", mount_entry_path(m), "/proc", opts, p->runtime_scope);
+        if (r != -EINVAL || !opts)
+                return r;
+
+        /* procfs rejects the whole option string over a single element it does not recognise, and both
+         * elements that can appear here arrived in the same release: the symbolic hidepid= values and
+         * subset= are Linux 5.8. Retry with the numeric hidepid= form and no subset=. */
+
+        const char *numeric = protect_proc_to_hidepid_numeric(p->protect_proc);
+        if (!numeric)
+                return r;
+
+        _cleanup_free_ char *numeric_opts = strjoin("hidepid=", numeric);
+        if (!numeric_opts)
+                return -ENOMEM;
+
+        if (streq(numeric_opts, opts)) /* Nothing left to downgrade; the kernel refused the numeric form. */
+                return r;
+
+        log_debug("Mounting procfs with '%s' failed with -EINVAL, retrying with '%s'%s.",
+                  opts, numeric_opts,
+                  p->proc_subset != PROC_SUBSET_ALL ? " and without subset=, which this kernel cannot honour" : "");
+
+        return mount_private_apivfs("proc", mount_entry_path(m), "/proc", numeric_opts, p->runtime_scope);
 }
 
